  "text": "comparative oncology – Flint Animal Cancer Center\nSkip to content\ncolorado state\nuniversity\nCSU\nflint animal\ncancer center\nAbout Us\nOur Story\nFACC Team\nContact Us\nNews\nFor Owners\nOur Services\nCancer Information Request\nAppointments\nTreatment Options\nPreparing for Your Visit\nFrequently Asked Questions\nFor DVMs\nConsult Line\nTreatment Options\nOne Cure\nAbout One Cure\nRacing for One Cure\nDonate to One Cure\nClinical Trials\nAbout Clinical Trials\nCurrent Clinical Trials\nEducation & Research\nComparative Oncology Research\nOncology Specialty Training\nDonate\nGreatest Need\nPatient Assistance\nOne Cure\nScholarship\nResearch\nFeline Cancer Research\nFACC Friends\nmenu\nclose\nTag:\ncomparative oncology\nsearch\nread more\nOne Cure Clinical Trials\nOne Health. One Cancer. One Cure.\nLauren Mingus\n|\nJanuary 23, 2020\nLate last year, the U.S. Senate designated January as One Health Awareness Month. According to the One Health Initiative, the vision of One Health is to improve the lives of all species—human and animal—through the integration of human medicine, veterinary medicine, and environmental science. Translational medicine, including comparative oncology, falls under the One Health umbrella. […]\nread more\nOne Cure Clinical Trials\nOur Dogs May Be The Answer To Cancer\nLauren Mingus\n|\nSeptember 30, 2019\nScientists dis­covered long ago that cancer is cancer. It doesn’t care if you have two legs or four – it looks the same under the microscope, spreads to the same locations, and reacts the same way to treatment. Despite advances in knowledge, cancer remains the leading cause of death in dogs beyond middle age and […]\nread more\nLatest News\nFlint Animal Cancer Center Featured On Canadian Television W5\nCTV-W5\n|\nFebruary 4, 2019\nThe Canadian television show, W5, featured the Flint Animal Cancer Center’s work in comparative oncology research.\nbrowse articles by topic\nFACC Patient Stories\nLatest News\nOne Cure Clinical Trial